abstract:
Let $A_N$ denote nondeterministic automatic complexity and \[ L_{k,c}=\{x\in [k]^* : A_N(x)> |x|/c\}. \] In particular, $L_{k,2}$ is the language of all $k$-ary words for which $A_N$ is maximal, while $L_{k,3}$ gives a rough dividing line between complex and simple. Let $\mathbf{CFL}$ denote the complexity class consisting of all context-free languages. While it is not known that $L_{2,2}$ is infinite, Kjos-Hanssen (2017) showed that $L_{3,2}$ is $\mathbf{CFL}$-immune but not $\mathbf{coCFL}$-immune. We complete the picture by showing that $L_{3,2}
ot\in\mathbf{coCFL}$. Turning to Boolean circuit complexity, we show that $L_{2,3}$ is $\mathbf{SAC}^0$-immune and $\mathbf{SAC}^0$-coimmune. Here $\mathbf{SAC}^0$ denotes the complexity class consisting of all languages computed by (non-uniform) constant-depth circuits with semi-unbounded fanin. As for arithmetic circuits, we show that $\{x:A_N(x)>1\}
ot\in\oplus\mathbf{SAC}^0$. In particular, $\mathbf{SAC}^0
ot\subseteq\oplus \mathbf{SAC}^0$, which resolves an open implication from the Complexity Zoo.
